/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-109090" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-109090" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}+4"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nCalculate $P({X}^{2}=9).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's first add an ${X}^{2}$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $9$ | $81$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third rows of our table, we can immediately conclude that \n\n$$\nP({X}^{2}=9)=0.35+0.1=0.45.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c388c1ed8737218b3f157b217ff4731b172ace8aed19307b31a77aa2ed9997c6" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.15"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.25"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.35"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.45"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ gets $36%$ of the copies.\n\n- Distributor $2$ gets $23%$ of the copies, and $92%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ gets $41%$ of the copies, and $95%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $3%$ of all copies have some printing defect, $70%$ of defective copies are sold, and $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is given to distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.36 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.23 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.92 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.41 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.36+0.23+0.41=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|S)=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold, which is represented as $P(S|{D}_{1}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\ P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.03)}{P(S|{D}_{1})(0.36)+(0.92)(0.23)+(0.95)(0.41)} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{D}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.025(0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011) & =0.021 \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.025} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011 \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.36}(\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011) \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & ≈0.664\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c388c1ed8737218b3f157b217ff4731b172ace8aed19307b31a77aa2ed9997c6" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.664"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.872"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.885"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.923"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.943"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(2.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(3.6\\times {10}^{-3})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(2.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (2.8\\times {10}^{6})}{3.6\\times {10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 4.5\\cdot 2.8}{3.6}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{{10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =23.345\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{23.345\\times {10}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{23.345}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{4}} \\\\  & =(4.8316…)\\times {10}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,4.8\\times {10}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c388c1ed8737218b3f157b217ff4731b172ace8aed19307b31a77aa2ed9997c6" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336300" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336300" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Three baskets, $A,B,$ and $C,$ contain pears. It is known that $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the pears from basket $A$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ of those from basket $B$ are ripe. A basket is selected at random, and a pear is chosen at random from the basket. If the probability that the selected pear is ripe is $\\frac{1}{4},$  what proportion of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be the event that the pear is ripe, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a ripe pear, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from baskets $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{5},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,P(R|B)=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from one of the baskets is $\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(R|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{15}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{180} \\\\ P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{23}{60}$ of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category