emy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-135395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inverse of the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-135395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we're given a Frobenius matrix:\n\n- all entries on the main diagonal are ones\n\n- the matrix has only one column (the $1$st column) where some entries below the main diagonal are not zeros\n\n- all the other entries outside the main diagonal are zeros\n\nTo find the inverse of a Frobenius matrix, we simply take the non-zero entries outside the main diagonal with opposite signs. \n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-135395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c388c1ed8737218b3f157b217ff4731b172ace8aed19307b31a77aa2e