/question-capture/14070542/assets/a77fcb19143b7bdee347993a1da4fb6f73c5c1460c55ecab24ffde9afe7f6cf0.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/c5e335ffe5f2343671d1f9eca6cedc34ef34a9700837451e963cfcfe1cd153d5.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/eb621888b04d5bd9b842624c278422995db44097adde61bbc769e8df0a5a47e7.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/d4e5b78932f8ff6155ac81e12538eb31ac211c9d27d6a2b0c67542527ccd3b2a.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2>0$ | $0$ | $-2<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =4(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+18 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-{x}^{2}+36 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-(4)^{2}+8(4)+36}=2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c388c1ed8737218b3f157b217ff4731b172ace8aed19307b31a77aa2ed9997c6" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "6\\,m/s"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "8\\,m/s"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The volume $X(t)$ (in $\\text{L}$) of water in a container as it fills toward its maximum capacity can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial volume is $1.5\\,\\text{L},$ how long does it take for the container to reach a volume of $2\\,\\text{L}?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that the container's volume is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dX}{3-X} & =0.4\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dX}{3-X} & =\\int 0.4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-X| & =0.4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $3-X>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $X(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-X & ={e}^{-0.4t-C} \\\\ 3-X & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ 3-X & =K{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ X & =3-K{e}^{-0.4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial volume was $1.5\\,\\text{L}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $X(0)=1.5,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5 & =3-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 1.5 & =3-K \\\\ K & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nX(t)=3-1.5{e}^{-0.4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the volume is $2\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X({t}_{1}) & =2 \\\\ 3-1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ 1 & =1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -0.4{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{0.4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =1.013… \\\\  & ≈1\\,\\text{minute},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c388c1ed8737218b3f157b217ff4731b172ace8aed19307b31a77aa2ed9997c6" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$1$ minute"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$2$ minutes"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$3$ minutes"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$4$ minutes"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$5$ minutes"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $y(t)$ of Weddell seals in a certain region of Antarctica follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1.2y-0.002{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. According to this model, what is the maximum possible seal population?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.2y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of seals cannot exceed $600.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c388c1ed8737218b3f157b217ff4731b172ace8aed19307b31a77aa2ed9997c6" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "1\\,200"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "300"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "500"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "600"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "850"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-12669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/e92694d3fc8d6be220fc4d45c0d210a53076db72daad477c3199ece5c4460627.png)\n\nIdentify the equation that generated the graph above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-12669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/51bf87385bc52632a0bf722d5672047e79afc8ea0556dabda3257c9ebd1e91a3.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $3$ units **down** to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-12669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-12669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-12669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+3"] ["math" "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3"] ["math" "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+3)"] ["math" "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-3)"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-12669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We split the integral into the sum of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0)+\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)+\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-114209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12(3\\pi +2)"] ["math" "6(3\\pi -2)"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi +2}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{9\\pi -4}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-340441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-340441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We split the integral into the difference of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+2\\,\\text{arccot}\\,x\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})+2(\\text{arccot}(1)-\\text{arccot}(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+2(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-340441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-340441" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-340441" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-4016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-2y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-4016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x-2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2y}} \\\\ {e}^{2y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{2y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2y} & ={e}^{x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{2y} & =2{e}^{x}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{0}+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+C) & =0 \\\\ 2+C & =1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-2y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-4016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-4016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-4016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1)"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{e}^{x}-3)"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-{e}^{x})"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{x}+1}{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-4016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $y(t)$ of Weddell seals in a certain region of Antarctica follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1.2y-0.002{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. According to this model, what is the maximum possible seal population?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.2y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of seals cannot exceed $600.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\,200"] ["math" "300"] ["math" "500"] ["math" "600"] ["math" "850"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-118552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "600"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-80778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface ${2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}=2.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,-1,1),$ what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-80778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=2.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})k \\\\  & =({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)i+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)j+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(y\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }i+x\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }j+3z\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }k) \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2((-1)\\cdot \\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }i+1\\cdot \\,\\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }j+3(1)\\cdot {2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-{2}^{1}\\text{ }i+{2}^{1}\\text{ }j+3\\cdot {2}^{1}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-i+j+3\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-80778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-80778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-80778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-80778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-337570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-337570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\ y+x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/98159dfb60fd280468ad8165475b5ba58e41658174a932b4c9555ef1153a577c.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-337570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-337570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-337570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=Ky"] ["math" "xy=K"] ["math" "y=Kx"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K"] ["math" "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-337570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-89885" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x+y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(-1,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(-1,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-89885" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-1,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =1+(x+y) \\\\  & =x+y+1 \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y+1)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(-1,1).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)}>0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-89885" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-89885" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-89885" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-89885" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-32522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4\\,i+2\\,k$ \nand \n$c=i+j-k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-32522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i+j-k)\\times (4\\,i+2\\,k) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(2+4)j+(0-4)k \\\\  & =2i-6j-4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-32522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-32522" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-32522" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2i-2j+4k"] ["math" "2i+2j-4k"] ["math" "2i-6j-4k"] ["math" "4i-6j+2k"] ["math" "i+6j+2k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-32522" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2i-6j-4k"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-74964" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-74964" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x(2x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{2x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot 0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-74964" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-74964" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-74964" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-74964" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-43780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ and its subset $A={f\\in C[-1,1]\\,|\\,f(1)\\le 0}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under function addition\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $A$ is a subspace of $C[-1,1]$\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-1,1]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-43780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(1) & =f(1)+g(1)\\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)\\in A$ and $k=-1\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & \\le 0 \\\\ kf(1) & \\ge k\\cdot 0 \\\\ (kf) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $A$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-43780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-43780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-43780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-43780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and IV only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-142950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-142950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)=\\frac{P(X=2,Y=y)}{P(X=2)}=\\frac{f(2,y)}{{f}_{X}(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in {2,4},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(2,4)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.4}{0.6}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-142950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-142950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-142950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |"] ["text" "| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |"] ["text" "| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |"] ["text" "| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{5}{6}$ |"] ["text" "| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-142950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-157468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x+y-2z=3$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-157468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3-y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z & =3 \\\\ (0)+(0)-2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z & =3 \\\\ (5)+(1)-2(0) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-157468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-157468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-157468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-157468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "IV only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-73243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t{e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-73243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((1-t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-2){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=2.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(1-(2)){e}^{-(2)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-73243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-73243" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-73243" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{1}{e}"] ["math" "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}"] ["math" "t=2,\\,v=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}"] ["math" "t=3,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}"] ["math" "t=3,\\,v=\\frac{3}{{e}^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-73243" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-109300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(t-3){e}^{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-109300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-3){e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+(t-3){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-2){e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+(t-2){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1){e}^{t} & =0 \\\\ t-1 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1)=(1-2){e}^{1}=-e.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-109300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-109300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-109300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=1,\\,v=-3e"] ["math" "t=1,\\,v=-e"] ["math" "t=1,\\,v=2e"] ["math" "t=2,\\,v=-2e"] ["math" "t=2,\\,v=e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-109300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=1,\\,v=-e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -12 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -12 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-42579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{21}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{13}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-157213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the section of the  parabola $3{y}^{2}-4y-16x=0$ that lies in the first quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=8$ on the parabola at the point where $x=2,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-157213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-4y-16x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{y}^{2}-4y-16x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{y}^{2}-4y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3{y}^{2}-4y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(16x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6y-4)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=8$ and $x=2.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot (6y-4)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=2$ into the equation of the  parabola. \n\nAt the point where $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-4y-16x & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-4y-16\\cdot (2) & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-4y-32 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)(3y+8) & =0 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y>0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $y=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot (6\\cdot (4)-4)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0. \\\\ 160-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0. \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-157213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-157213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-157213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-157213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-313939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the section of the parabola ${x}^{2}-4y=5$ that lies in the first quadrant.  Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=8$ on the parabola at the point where $y=11,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-313939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4y & =5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}-4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=8$ and $y=11,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-8\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-32 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=11$ into the equation of the parabola.  \n\nAt the point where $y=11,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4y & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4(11) & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-44 & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =49 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $x=7,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-32 & =0 \\\\ 2(7)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-32 & =0 \\\\ 14\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{32}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-313939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-313939" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-313939" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{16}{7}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-161651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $A=∂A\\cup \\text{int}A$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-161651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/a39ecae41a88e873fa9eecb30a5b554be9ec98cf630851715ab35f2f873710f2.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and III are false. Since ${C}_{1}⊂∂A-A,$ we must have that $∂A⊄A$ and also that $A\\ne ∂A\\cup \\text{int}A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-161651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-161651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-161651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-161651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-96055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\int (2{csch}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-96055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t & \\,⇒\\,\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡t)=-{csch}^{2}⁡t & \\,⇒\\,\\int {csch}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{csch}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\int {csch}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-96055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-96055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-96055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C"] ["math" "-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C"] ["math" "-2{coth}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-96055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-95505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{e}^{x},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-95505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{({e}^{x})}^{2}}\\cdot {({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{e}^{2x}}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-95505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-95505" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-95505" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}+1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{1-{e}^{x}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+{e}^{2x}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-95505" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-111083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y<2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-111083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y<2) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(0,1),(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(0,1))+P((X,Y)=(1,1)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(1,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,1)) \\\\  & =f(0,1)+f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(2,1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.25+0.1+0.25 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-111083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-111083" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-111083" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.25"] ["math" "0.35"] ["math" "0.6"] ["math" "0.65"] ["math" "0.7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-111083" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-32649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $C=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $C$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-32649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $C$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & -3 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,0\\,\\, & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{32}=-1$ and ${u}_{33}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=(-1)\\cdot 7=-7.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-32649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-32649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-32649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-7"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-32649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-338002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $D(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+2y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y,$ then $D({x}^{3}-x)=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-338002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}=x,{f}_{2}=2y,{f}_{1}=0,$ and ${f}_{0}=1.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={x}^{3}-x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({x}^{3}-x) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({x}^{3}-x)+2({x}^{3}-x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{3}-x)+({x}^{3}-x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(3{x}^{2}-1)+2({x}^{3}-x)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-1)+({x}^{3}-x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x)+2({x}^{3}-x)\\cdot (6x)+({x}^{3}-x) \\\\  & =x\\cdot 6+12x({x}^{3}-x)+({x}^{3}-x) \\\\  & =12{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-338002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-338002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-338002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12{x}^{3}-{8}^{2}+5x"] ["math" "12{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+5x"] ["math" "12{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+5x"] ["math" "8{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+7x"] ["math" "8{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+3x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-338002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+5x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-338188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ then $D({e}^{x})=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-338188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}=y,{f}_{2}=0,{f}_{1}=-xy,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={e}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({e}^{x}) & ={e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({e}^{x})-x({e}^{x})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{x})-x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(1-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-338188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-338188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-338188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{e}^{2x}(1+x)"] ["math" "{e}^{2x}(1-x)"] ["math" "{e}^{2x}(2-x)"] ["math" "{e}^{2x}(x-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-338188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{2x}(1-x)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-131502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-131502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-3y+2z=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx-3y+2z=-1\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=3y-2z-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3y-2z-1 \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y,z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-131502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-131502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-131502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1-3y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1-3y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}y-2z-3 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["text" "No solution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-131502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$K=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\,-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(K) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\,-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd column of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(K) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(2-0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-42956" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42956" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42956" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-85172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(10,5),(0,-5),$ and $(15,20).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(9,5)$\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(10,-7)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-85172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{35-30}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 50 & -125 \\\\ 5 & -35 & 95\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 10 & -25 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 19\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(0,1),(10,-7),(-25,19).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-85172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-85172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-85172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-85172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-9253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(-1,0)$\n- $(0,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-9253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-2-0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(-1,0),(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-9253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-9253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-9253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-9253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-130455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices which of the following statements are always true? \n\n- $A(BC)=(AB)C$\n- $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- $CA=AC$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-130455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $A(BC)=(AB)C$ is always true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B-C)=AB-AC,$ but $AC\\ne CA$ because matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\n\n- Statement III  is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $CA\\ne AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement  I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-130455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-130455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-130455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-130455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-15664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/e4b9bd92b63d85b0336974285b6aa7a8a7beb8348cf368f65d805f7b4b70ffa2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-15664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(1)=3$ and $g(1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (0,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (0,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-15664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-15664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-15664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-15664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-18597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/7a650dd6ba3afd925a8a5672435244d454dcc06d3ac72da811f3cc2867e15023.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(0)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-18597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(0)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(0)\\cdot g(0)-f(0)\\cdot {g}^{′}(0)}{(g(0))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(0)=1$ and $g(0)=1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,2)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ equals $1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(0)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(0)\\cdot g(0)-f(0)\\cdot {g}^{′}(0)}{(g(0))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-18597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-18597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-18597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-18597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-75825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-75825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x-8=(x+2)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8} & =\\frac{3x-6}{(x+2)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n3x-6=A(x-4)+B(x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4)-6 & =A(4-4)+B(4+2) \\\\ 6 & =6B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-2)-6 & =A(-2-4)+B(-2+2) \\\\ -12 & =-6A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}=\\frac{2}{x+2}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x+2}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{2}(x-4)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{2}(x-4)|{|}_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-75825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-75825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-75825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] ["math" "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-75825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-135395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inverse of the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-135395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we're given a Frobenius matrix:\n\n- all entries on the main diagonal are ones\n\n- the matrix has only one column (the $1$st column) where some entries below the main diagonal are not zeros\n\n- all the other entries outside the main diagonal are zeros\n\nTo find the inverse of a Frobenius matrix, we simply take the non-zero entries outside the main diagonal with opposite signs. \n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-135395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-135395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-135395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-135395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-130807" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-130807" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-8){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}49 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-130807" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-130807" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-130807" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-49 & -8 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 49\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 49\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}49 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-130807" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}49 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-71876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $CD=DC={I}_{3}$\n- $CE=EC={I}_{3}$\n- $D={C}^{-1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-71876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- First, we check whether $CD=DC={I}_{3}.$ Computing $CD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $DC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DC & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $D={C}^{-1}$ and statement III is true too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $CE=EC={I}_{3}.$ Computing $CE$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CE & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 3 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-71876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-71876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-71876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-71876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-11636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AC=CA={I}_{3}$\n- $AD=DA={I}_{3}$\n- $D={A}^{-1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-11636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- First, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AD=DA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 7 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false. As a result, $D\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-11636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-11636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-11636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I  only"] ["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-11636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I  only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-77433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $A(1,1,0)$ and $B(3,-1,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-77433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $A$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=a+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $a$ is the position vector of $A.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $A(1,1,0)$ and $B(3,-1,2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,2⟩-⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-77433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-77433" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-77433" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-77433" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-19777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $(2a+3c)\\times (b+2c)$, if $a\\times b=3i-2j+k$, $a\\times c=j-2k$ and $b\\times c=2i-3j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-19777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall that:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2a+3c)\\times (b+2c) & = \\\\ ((2a)\\times b)+((2a)\\times (2c))+((3c)\\times b)+((3c)\\times (2c))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((2a)\\times b)+((2a)\\times (2c))+((3c)\\times b)+((3c)\\times (2c)) & = \\\\ 2(a\\times b)+(2\\cdot 2)(a\\times c)+3(c\\times b)+(3\\cdot 2)(c\\times c) & = \\\\ 2(a\\times b)+4(a\\times c)+3(c\\times b)+6(c\\times c)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $c\\times c=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(a\\times b)+4(a\\times c)+3(c\\times b)+6(c\\times c) & = \\\\ 2(a\\times b)+4(a\\times c)-3(b\\times c)+6(0) & = \\\\ 2(a\\times b)+4(a\\times c)-3(b\\times c)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(a\\times b)+4(a\\times c)-3(b\\times c) & = \\\\ 2(3i-2j+k)+4(j-2k)-3(2i-3j) & = \\\\ (6i-4j+2k)+(4j-8k)-(6i-9j) & = \\\\ (6i-6i)+(-4j+4j+9j)+(2k-8k) & = \\\\ 9j-6k\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-19777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-19777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-19777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-9j+6k"] ["math" "6i-2j"] ["math" "6j-3k"] ["math" "9j+2k"] ["math" "9j-6k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-19777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9j-6k"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-127861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $(3b-c)\\times (9a-2b)$, if $a\\times b=⟨0,0,1⟩$, $a\\times c=⟨0,-1,3⟩$ and $c\\times b=⟨0,2,0⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-127861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall that:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3b-c)\\times (9a-2b) & = \\\\ ((3b)\\times (9a))-((3b)\\times (2b))-(c\\times (9a))+(c\\times (2b))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3b-c)\\times (9a-2b) & = \\\\ ((3b)\\times (9a))-((3b)\\times (2b))-(c\\times (9a))+(c\\times (2b)) & = \\\\ (3\\cdot 9)(b\\times a)-(3\\cdot 2)(b\\times b)-9(c\\times a)+2(c\\times b) & = \\\\ 27(b\\times a)-6(b\\times b)-9(c\\times a)+2(c\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $b\\times b=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27(b\\times a)-6(b\\times b)-9(c\\times a)+2(c\\times b) & = \\\\ -27(a\\times b)-6(0)+9(a\\times c)+2(c\\times b) & = \\\\ -27(a\\times b)+9(a\\times c)+2(c\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-27(a\\times b)+9(a\\times c)+2(c\\times b) & = \\\\ -27⟨0,0,1⟩+9⟨0,-1,3⟩+2⟨0,2,0⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨0,0,-27⟩+⟨0,-9,27⟩+⟨0,4,0⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨0,-5,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-127861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-127861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-127861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-5,0,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨-5,0,10⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,-5,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,5,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,5,10⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-127861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,-5,0⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-77159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(2,4,1),$ $Q(3,4,2)$ and $R(3,3,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-77159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $P,$ $Q$ and $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨2,4,1⟩,\\,q=⟨3,4,2⟩,\\,r=⟨3,3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨2,4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨3,3,1⟩-⟨2,4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-77159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-77159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-77159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{30}^{∘}"] ["math" "{45}^{∘}"] ["math" "{60}^{∘}"] ["math" "{75}^{∘}"] ["math" "{90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-77159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{60}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-101506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(1,5,-1)$, $Q(5,1,1)$, and $R(2,5,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-101506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨1,5,-1⟩,\\,q=⟨5,1,1⟩,\\,r=⟨2,5,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨5,1,1⟩-⟨1,5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,5,0⟩-⟨1,5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-101506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-101506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-101506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{0}^{∘}"] ["math" "{30}^{∘}"] ["math" "{45}^{∘}"] ["math" "{60}^{∘}"] ["math" "{90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-101506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{45}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-181704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set ${2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}}$ given below.\n\n${2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}}={z\\in C\\,:\\,{z}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n${z}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-181704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given set contains the roots $z\\in C$ of some cubic polynomial $p(z).$\n\nSince the cubic polynomial has roots ${z}_{1}=2,$ ${z}_{2}=3-\\text{i},$ and ${z}_{3}=3+\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(z) & =a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2})(z-{z}_{3}) \\\\  & =(1)(z-2)(z-(3-\\text{i}))(z-(3+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(z-2)({z}^{2}-z(3+\\text{i})-z(3-\\text{i})+(3-\\text{i})(3+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(z-2)({z}^{2}-6z+10) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-6{z}^{2}+10z-2{z}^{2}+12z-20 \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-8{z}^{2}+22z-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as\n\n$$\n{2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}}={z\\in C\\,:\\,{z}^{3}-8{z}^{2}+22z-20=0}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-181704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-181704" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-181704" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-181704" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-181704" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "22"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-181704" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-181704" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-105109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let\n$a=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ x \\\\ 9 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $a\\cdot b=-3$, find the value of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-105109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Starting from the equation $a\\cdot b=-3,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-3 \\\\ x\\cdot 3+2\\cdot x+1\\cdot 9+(-1)\\cdot 7 & =-3 \\\\ 3x+2x+9-7 & =-3 \\\\ 5x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-105109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-105109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-105109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-105109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114473" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\int (8+8{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114473" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ with $\\theta =4x+3,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3)={csc}^{2}⁡(4x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (8+8{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x & =8\\int (1+{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int {csc}^{2}⁡(4x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8[-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)]+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114473" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-114473" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114473" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C"] ["math" "-8\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+3)+C"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+3)+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114473" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-10567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\int (7+4{x}^{3}+7{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-10567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =3x-2\\pi ,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (7+4{x}^{3}+7{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+7+7{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+7[1+{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi )])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+7{csc}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+7(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))+C \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-10567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-10567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-10567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C"] ["math" "{x}^{4}+\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C"] ["math" "{x}^{4}-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C"] ["math" "{x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C"] ["math" "{x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-10567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-126031" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute $P(X>2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-126031" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =P(2<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{x+1}]}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-126031" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-126031" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-126031" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-126031" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-73551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 5x-15y=10 \\\\  & 2x-4y=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-73551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-73551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-73551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-73551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-73551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-80657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=-5,\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=7,\n$$\n\nfind the value of \n\n$$\n{∬}_{C\\cup D}4f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-80657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}4f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =4(-5+7) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-80657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-80657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-80657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-8"] ["math" "28"] ["math" "40"] ["math" "48"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-80657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-17552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}={y}^{2}-y\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution* $z={y}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-17552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ -\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-1+z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-1,\\,Q(x)=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-x}$ yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}\\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx}-z) & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot (-1) \\\\ {e}^{-x}\\frac{dz}{dx}-{e}^{-x}z & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-x}) & =-{e}^{-x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-x})\\,dx\\, & =-\\int {e}^{-x}\\,dx \\\\ z{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-x}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =1+C{e}^{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-17552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-17552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-17552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{x+C}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{-1}{1+C{e}^{x}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{-1+Cx}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{-1+C{e}^{x}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-17552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-98525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+y-2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-98525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,1).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-98525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-98525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-98525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/0b6a1907f045e737c1f136a47d941069efbfd23a389add17d1ae31cf336763b8.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/a77fcb19143b7bdee347993a1da4fb6f73c5c1460c55ecab24ffde9afe7f6cf0.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/c5e335ffe5f2343671d1f9eca6cedc34ef34a9700837451e963cfcfe1cd153d5.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/eb621888b04d5bd9b842624c278422995db44097adde61bbc769e8df0a5a47e7.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-98525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/d4e5b78932f8ff6155ac81e12538eb31ac211c9d27d6a2b0c67542527ccd3b2a.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2>0$ | $0$ | $-2<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =4(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+18 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-{x}^{2}+36 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-(4)^{2}+8(4)+36}=2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s"] ["math" "6\\,m/s"] ["math" "8\\,m/s"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-163548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-127727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-127727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =|\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}|\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{2({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s={e}^{t}-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}-1 & =s \\\\ {e}^{t} & =s+1 \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-(s+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,(s+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{s+1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-127727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-127727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-127727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "R(s)=\\frac{s+1}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡s),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡s)⟩"] ["math" "R(s)=\\frac{s+1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(s+1),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s+1)⟩"] ["math" "R(s)=\\frac{s+1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩"] ["math" "R(s)=\\frac{s}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-1)],\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-1)]⟩"] ["math" "R(s)=\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡s),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡s)⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-127727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "R(s)=\\frac{s+1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The volume $X(t)$ (in $\\text{L}$) of water in a container as it fills toward its maximum capacity can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial volume is $1.5\\,\\text{L},$ how long does it take for the container to reach a volume of $2\\,\\text{L}?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that the container's volume is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dX}{3-X} & =0.4\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dX}{3-X} & =\\int 0.4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-X| & =0.4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $3-X>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $X(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-X & ={e}^{-0.4t-C} \\\\ 3-X & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ 3-X & =K{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ X & =3-K{e}^{-0.4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial volume was $1.5\\,\\text{L}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $X(0)=1.5,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5 & =3-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 1.5 & =3-K \\\\ K & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nX(t)=3-1.5{e}^{-0.4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the volume is $2\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X({t}_{1}) & =2 \\\\ 3-1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ 1 & =1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -0.4{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{0.4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =1.013… \\\\  & ≈1\\,\\text{minute},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$1$ minute"] ["text" "$2$ minutes"] ["text" "$3$ minutes"] ["text" "$4$ minutes"] ["text" "$5$ minutes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-335282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$1$ minute"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-335280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A can of soda is taken out of a refrigerator and left in a room that stays at a constant temperature of $20{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ The soda’s temperature $T(t)$ (in degrees Celsius) can be modeled by the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes, and $k>0$ is a constant. The initial temperature of the soda is $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and after $5$ minutes, the soda has warmed to $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the soda to reach $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-335280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that the temperature of the can is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T).\n$$\n\nWe separate variables and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{20-T} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{20-T} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20-T| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $20-T>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20-T & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 20-T & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 20-T & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=8,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =20-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 8 & =20-K \\\\ K & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=20-12{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the temperature of the soda was $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=12,$  we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =20-12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 8 & =12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ {e}^{-5k} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -5k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$  be the time when the soda can reaches $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =16 \\\\ 20-12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =16 \\\\ 4 & =12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the value of $k,$ we get:\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}=13.547…≈14\\text{ }\\text{minutes},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-335280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-335280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-335280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$10$ minutes"] ["text" "$12$ minutes"] ["text" "$14$ minutes"] ["text" "$19$ minutes"] ["text" "$8$ minutes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-335280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$14$ minutes"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-107499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $z=\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-107499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1)^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =3(-1-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=-3-3\\text{i},$ the magnitude is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ are both negative, $z$ lies in the third quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-107499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-107499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-107499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=2\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))"] ["math" "z=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))"] ["math" "z=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))"] ["math" "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))"] ["math" "z=\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-107499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-141281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-141281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-141281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-141281" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-141281" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-141281" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-65502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-65502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-65502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-65502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-65502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-65502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-119865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-119865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-119865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-119865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-119865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-119865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-95024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The time that a particular laptop battery lasts is normally distributed with a mean of $32$ hours and a standard deviation of $4$ hours. What is the probability that a full charge lasts for $36$ hours or more? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-95024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $X$ represent the time the full charge lasts. Then we have $X∼N(32,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X\\ge 36).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 36) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{36-32}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 1) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1)=1-0.8413≈0.1587.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a full charge lasts $36$ hours or more is approximately $0.16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-95024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-95024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-95024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.13"] ["math" "0.16"] ["math" "0.23"] ["math" "0.27"] ["math" "0.31"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-95024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-120300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,-11).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-120300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-11)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121} \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the negative half of $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(11,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-120300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-120300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-120300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(11,0)"] ["math" "(11,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "(11,\\pi )"] ["math" "(\\frac{11}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{11},\\frac{\\pi }{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-120300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(11,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-109090" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=0,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4].$ \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-109090" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=0.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot f(-1)+{0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-109090" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-109090" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-109090" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}+4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-109090" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-79870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation ${log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4)={log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-79870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & ={log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =-3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡((-2)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3(-2)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(8) & ={log}_{4}⁡(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡((-1)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3(-1)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(5) & ={log}_{4}⁡(5).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2,-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-79870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-79870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-79870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ only"] ["text" "$x=-2$ and $x=-1$"] ["text" "$x=-2$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=-2$ only"] ["text" "$x=1$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-79870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-2$ and $x=-1$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-110799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}+4)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-110799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =2x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({0}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(0)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(8) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0,2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-110799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-110799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-110799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-2$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ and  $x=-2$"] ["text" "$x=0$ and  $x=2$"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=2$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-110799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=0$ and  $x=2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nCalculate $P({X}^{2}=9).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's first add an ${X}^{2}$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $9$ | $81$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third rows of our table, we can immediately conclude that \n\n$$\nP({X}^{2}=9)=0.35+0.1=0.45.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.1"] ["math" "0.15"] ["math" "0.25"] ["math" "0.35"] ["math" "0.45"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-110220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.45"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-115511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,4)$ and which passes through the point $(4,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-115511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,1),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(1-4) \\\\ 4 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-115511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-115511" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-115511" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+2)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}(y+4)"] ["math" "(x+2)^{2}=3(y+4)"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}(y-4)"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)"] ["math" "(x-4)^{2}=4(y-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-115511" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-87562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $9$ data points, where\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-87562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=9$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 117 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =13-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =13-9 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-87562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-87562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-87562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-87562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-78257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write the equation of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}=40$ in standard form." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-78257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $40$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{40}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{40} & =\\frac{40}{40} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-78257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-78257" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-78257" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-78257" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-26387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in terms of ${log}_{9},$ what value of $y$ satisfies the equation ${9}^{2y+1}={5}^{-y}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-26387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take ${log}_{9}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2y+1} & ={5}^{-y} \\\\ {log}_{9}⁡({9}^{2y+1}) & ={log}_{9}⁡({5}^{-y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n2y+1={log}_{9}⁡({5}^{-y})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n2y+1=-y{log}_{9}⁡(5)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =-y{log}_{9}⁡(5) \\\\ 2y+y{log}_{9}⁡(5) & =-1 \\\\ y(2+{log}_{9}⁡(5)) & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}⁡(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-26387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-26387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-26387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "2+{log}_{9}⁡(5)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2-{log}_{9}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{log}_{9}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "{log}_{9}⁡(5)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-26387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}⁡(5)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ gets $36%$ of the copies.\n\n- Distributor $2$ gets $23%$ of the copies, and $92%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ gets $41%$ of the copies, and $95%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $3%$ of all copies have some printing defect, $70%$ of defective copies are sold, and $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is given to distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.36 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.23 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.92 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.41 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.36+0.23+0.41=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|S)=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold, which is represented as $P(S|{D}_{1}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\ P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.03)}{P(S|{D}_{1})(0.36)+(0.92)(0.23)+(0.95)(0.41)} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{D}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.025(0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011) & =0.021 \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.025} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011 \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.36}(\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011) \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & ≈0.664\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.664"] ["math" "0.872"] ["math" "0.885"] ["math" "0.923"] ["math" "0.943"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-148669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.664"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-336300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(2.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(3.6\\times {10}^{-3})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-336300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(2.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (2.8\\times {10}^{6})}{3.6\\times {10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 4.5\\cdot 2.8}{3.6}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{{10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =23.345\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{23.345\\times {10}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{23.345}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{4}} \\\\  & =(4.8316…)\\times {10}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,4.8\\times {10}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-336300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-336300" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-336300" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4.8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-336300" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-336300" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-143876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Three baskets, $A,B,$ and $C,$ contain pears. It is known that $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the pears from basket $A$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ of those from basket $B$ are ripe. A basket is selected at random, and a pear is chosen at random from the basket. If the probability that the selected pear is ripe is $\\frac{1}{4},$  what proportion of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-143876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be the event that the pear is ripe, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a ripe pear, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from baskets $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{5},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,P(R|B)=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from one of the baskets is $\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(R|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{15}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{180} \\\\ P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{23}{60}$ of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-143876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-143876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-143876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{17}{50}"] ["math" "\\frac{18}{55}"] ["math" "\\frac{19}{62}"] ["math" "\\frac{23}{60}"] ["math" "\\frac{24}{55}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-143876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{23}{60}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In a particular city, about $30%$ of mornings are cloudy and $40%$ of days are rainy. \nIt is known that $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings. What is the probability that a randomly selected cloudy morning continues as a rainy day?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected morning is cloudy, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected day is rainy. Then, we require $P(R|C),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(R|C)=\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)}\n$$\n\n- $P(C)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of mornings are cloudy.\n\n- $P(R)=0.4,$ since $40%$ of days are rainy.\n\n- $P(C|R)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|C) & =\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6\\cdot 0.4}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-42567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.25"] ["math" "0.45"] ["math" "0.6"] ["math" "0.8"] ["math" "0.9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-42567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-76311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the least common multiple of $6w,$ $3{w}^{3},$ and $4{w}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-76311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three monomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable parts. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(6,3,4):$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,12,18,…$\n\n- Multiples of $3:$ $\\,3,6,9,12,…$\n\n- Multiples of $4:$ $\\,4,8,12,16,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(6,3,4)=12.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $w,\\,{w}^{3},$ and ${w}^{2},$ we raise $w$ to the largest power that appears in all the expressions, which is $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w,{w}^{3},{w}^{2})={w}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(6w,3{w}^{3},4{w}^{2})=12\\cdot {w}^{3}=12{w}^{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-76311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-76311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-76311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12{w}^{3}"] ["math" "12{w}^{6}"] ["math" "36{w}^{6}"] ["math" "72{w}^{3}"] ["math" "72{w}^{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-76311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12{w}^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114961" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/d624edaffd8c90de40041d419cf65bcf8f3af5d31c8d88d1123bd8f3d7555166.png)\n\nWhat is the length of the major axis of the ellipse given above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114961" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The major axis is the longest diameter of the ellipse, which is the line segment bounded by the points $(-3,1)$ and $(5,1).$ So, the length of the major axis is $5-(-3)=8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114961" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-114961" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114961" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-114961" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-86590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $U={2,4,6,8,10,12}$ is the universal set and $B={2,6,10},$ then the complement of $B$ is" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-86590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The complement of $B,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{B},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $B.$\n\nThe elements of $U$ that are not in $B$ are $4,$ $8,$   and $12.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $B$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{B}={4,8,12}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-86590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c32ec2b3bd557d"} {"question" "q-86590" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-86590" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{2,4,8,12}"] ["math" "{4,12}"] ["math" "{4,8,10,12}"] ["math" "{4,8,12}"] ["math" "{4,8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-86590" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{4,8,12}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "571de7e23b91d1fb7e427fc9ff8cb7cd33d5fa250fc19b8b3e5c65beeb2cd89c"} {"question" "q-12669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "1d9f1d484fb3686734a35a1c5043706af7f773bcaa1dd9ba8450c2f30cc155d6" "reviewer_session" "