ame{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))"] ["math" "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))"] ["math" "z=\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "1d9f1d484fb3686734a35a1c5043706af7f773bcaa1dd9ba8450c2f30cc155d6" "rationale" "Rationalizing 6/(−1+i) gives −3−3i, with magnitude 3√2 and third-quadrant argument 5π/4. This establishes original option 2. The complete saved choices, authentic worked solution, and Correct grade agree. The historical polar RHS is identical in value; the source adds z= and function-application markers."} {"question" "q-26387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}⁡(5)}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "1d9f1d484fb3686734a35a1c5043706af7f773bcaa1dd9ba8450c2f30cc155d6" "reviewer_session" "01a108cd-e7f4-73f3-b8fc-c82135bc6b7d" "rationale" "Taking log base 9 of 9^(2y+1)=5^(−y) gives 2y+1=−y log₉(5), hence y=−1/(2+log₉(5)), original option 1. The complete saved choices, authentic worked solution, and Correct grade agree. The historical expression has the same base, argument, signs, and denominator; the only difference is the MathML function-application marker." "bindings" {"problem" "1843b3ea5eeebe6f3b25be2c4138cd896135da7f63b72db2af11d517fa395935" "worked_solution" "0917b00e3add69d62e71ad57f9d322c9dd1a2eb393cc784998a4d8e0220994d7" "original_field" "72ab823f223424450c54b4b70ac2f84f09140e146dd98d1281a344eac2e44406" "reviewed_field" "6c64eb439ed5689df1bfed58dc8e14db28e19719266070e780f3c467e542a12b" "raw_widget" "bf963a41b99caafd6286e58efcd4a1afc4f657345180eda600782de4b3377c0