7592186257805 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e92694d3fc8d6be220fc4d45c0d210a53076db72daad477c3199ece5c4460627.png)\n\nIdentify the equation that generated the graph above."] [17592186257805 :question/worked-solution "To plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $3$ units **down** to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x-3$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x-3.$"] [17592186257805 :question/answer-fields 17592186274432] [17592186257116 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/d624edaffd8c90de40041d419cf65bcf8f3af5d31c8d88d1123bd8f3d7555166.png)\n\nWhat is the length of the major axis of the ellipse given above?"] [17592186256714 :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ).$"] [17592186256714 :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1)^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =3(-1-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=-3-3\\text{i},$ the magnitude is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}(z).$ Since $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ are both negative, $z$ lies in the third quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z) & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}1 \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))\n$$"] [17592186256714 :question/answer-fields 17592186274201] [17592186257410 :question/answer-fields 17592186274367] [17592186247199 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/e4b9bd92b63d85b0336974285b6aa7a8a7beb8348cf368f65d805f7b4b70ffa2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$"] [17592186269143 :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P,$ $Q$ and $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨2,4,1⟩,\\,q=⟨3,4,2⟩,\\,r=⟨3,3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964292/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨2,4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨3,3,1⟩-⟨2,4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$"] [17592186326486 :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨1,5,-1⟩,\\,q=⟨5,1,1⟩,\\,r=⟨2,5,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046481/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨5,1,1⟩-⟨1,5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,5,0⟩-⟨1,5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"] [17592186261938 :question/problem "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $9$ data points, where\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117.\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}"] [17592186261938 :question/answer-fields 17592186261939] [17592186261760 :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4)={log}_{4}(-3x+2).$"] [17592186261760 :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4) & ={log}_{4}(-3x+2) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =-3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}((-2)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3(-2)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}(8) & ={log}_{4}(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}((-1)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3(-1)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}(5) & ={log}_{4}(5).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2,-1.$"] [17592186261760 :question/answer-fields 17592186275031] [17592186256839 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4)=\\operatorname{log}(2x+4).$"] [17592186256839 :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & =\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =2x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({0}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2(0)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(4) & =\\operatorname{log}(4).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({2}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(8) & =\\operatorname{log}(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0,2.$"] [17592186256839 :question/answer-fields 17592186274237] [17592186257073 :question/worked-solution "We split the integral into the sum of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}t\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arctan}1-\\operatorname{arctan}0)+\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)+\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186257073 :question/answer-fields 17592186274305] [17592186294667 :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\ y+x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/98159dfb60fd280468ad8165475b5ba58e41658174a932b4c9555ef1153a577c.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$"] [17592186369217 :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,1).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png)"] [17592186261611 :question/answer-fields 17592186274998] [17592186369042 :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.2y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of seals cannot exceed $600.$"] [17592186294580 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/d4e5b78932f8ff6155ac81e12538eb31ac211c9d27d6a2b0c67542527ccd3b2a.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2>0$ | $0$ | $-2<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =4(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+18 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-{x}^{2}+36 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-(4)^{2}+8(4)+36}=2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s\n$$"] [17592186259101 :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{9}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2y+1} & ={5}^{-y} \\\\ {log}_{9}({9}^{2y+1}) & ={log}_{9}({5}^{-y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n2y+1={log}_{9}({5}^{-y})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n2y+1=-y{log}_{9}(5)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =-y{log}_{9}(5) \\\\ 2y+y{log}_{9}(5) & =-1 \\\\ y(2+{log}_{9}(5)) & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186259101 :question/answer-fields 17592186274598]] "immutable_answers" [17592186247201 17592186247202 17592186247203 17592186247204 17592186247205 17592186261940 17592186269145 17592186269146 17592186269147 17592186269148 17592186269149 17592186269632 17592186269633 17592186269634 17592186269635 17592186269636 17592186269810 17592186269811 17592186269812 17592186269813 17592186269814 17592186270147 17592186270148 17592186270149 17592186270150 17592186270151 17592186270457 17592186270458 17592186270459 17592186270460 17592186270461 17592186270782 17592186270783 17592186270784 17592186270785 17592186270786 17592186271602 17592186271603 17592186271604 17592186271605 17592186271606 17592186273571 17592186273572 17592186273573 17592186273574 17592186273575 17592186273736 17592186273737 17592186273738 17592186273739 17592186273740 17592186283866 17592186283867 17592186283868 17592186283869 17592186283870 17592186284297 17592186284298 17592186284299 17592186284300 17592186284301 17592186288245 17592186288246 17592186288247 17592186288248 17592186288249 17592186294215 17592186294216 17592186294217 17592186294218 17592186294219 17592186294221 17592186294222 17592186294223 17592186294224 17592186294225 17592186294227 17592186294228 17592186294229 17592186294230 17592186294231 17592186294233 17592186294234 17592186294235 17592186294236 17592186294237 17592186294260 17592186294261 17592186294262 17592186294263 17592186294264 17592186294266 17592186294267 17592186294